{"uid":"54d9fb8ada613311","name":"test_task_in_progress_visible_while_worker_iteration_running","fullName":"tests.test_e2e#test_task_in_progress_visible_while_worker_iteration_running","historyId":"400a8a41416fdeacfdf7e003c06c44ee","time":{"start":1791915807336,"stop":1791915808490,"duration":1154},"description":"`GET /tasks` re-reads `tasks.json` from disk on every call, so an\n    operator watching `ralphctl tasks` (or the hub) during an iteration\n    sees the exact task being worked *while the iteration is still\n    running* -- with no engine-side poll required (task 022, requirement\n    F: the engine's own `_poll_task_changes`, the 0.25s background poller\n    that used to re-read `tasks.json` purely to turn this into a live\n    `task` *event*, is retired). Since task 116, the harness itself claims\n    the task -- writes `in-progress` and emits the matching `task` event --\n    strictly BEFORE spawning the worker subprocess for iteration 2, so the\n    stub's `STUB_SLEEP` window (it does no file write of its own for this\n    any more; the harness's pre-spawn write already happened) gives this\n    test a real window in which the iteration is still running (no\n    meta.json endedAt yet) but tasks.json already shows in-progress.\n\n    The second half proves both halves of requirement F's rule at once:\n    a status never changes mid-hat (so the ONLY `task` events for 001 are\n    the harness's own pending->in-progress claim, which lands strictly\n    BEFORE iteration 2 even starts, and the pending->completed... no --\n    in-progress->completed move applied from the worker's JSON verdict,\n    which lands strictly AFTER iteration 2's `iteration.end`) -- there is\n    no third, mid-hat event for the transient state, because there is no\n    transient state left to observe: the harness already committed\n    in-progress before iteration 2's subprocess so much as started.","descriptionHtml":"<p><code>GET /tasks</code> re-reads <code>tasks.json</code> from disk on every call, so an<br />\noperator watching <code>ralphctl tasks</code> (or the hub) during an iteration<br />\nsees the exact task being worked <em>while the iteration is still<br />\nrunning</em> -- with no engine-side poll required (task 022, requirement<br />\nF: the engine's own <code>_poll_task_changes</code>, the 0.25s background poller<br />\nthat used to re-read <code>tasks.json</code> purely to turn this into a live<br />\n<code>task</code> <em>event</em>, is retired). Since task 116, the harness itself claims<br />\nthe task -- writes <code>in-progress</code> and emits the matching <code>task</code> event --<br />\nstrictly BEFORE spawning the worker subprocess for iteration 2, so the<br />\nstub's <code>STUB_SLEEP</code> window (it does no file write of its own for this<br />\nany more; the harness's pre-spawn write already happened) gives this<br />\ntest a real window in which the iteration is still running (no<br />\nmeta.json endedAt yet) but tasks.json already shows in-progress.</p>\n<pre><code>The second half proves both halves of requirement F's rule at once:\na status never changes mid-hat (so the ONLY `task` events for 001 are\nthe harness's own pending-&gt;in-progress claim, which lands strictly\nBEFORE iteration 2 even starts, and the pending-&gt;completed... no --\nin-progress-&gt;completed move applied from the worker's JSON verdict,\nwhich lands strictly AFTER iteration 2's `iteration.end`) -- there is\nno third, mid-hat event for the transient state, because there is no\ntransient state left to observe: the harness already committed\nin-progress before iteration 2's subprocess so much as started.\n</code></pre>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791915518558,"stop":1791915518558,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":fa